{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 227 10 "Problem
 3." }{TEXT 324 3 ".  " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 324 56 "There is a one-to-one correspondence between subset
s of " }{TEXT 228 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 324 28 " and binary \+
lists of length " }{TEXT 229 1 "n" }{TEXT 324 17 ", that is, lists " }
{TEXT 230 3 "L =" }{TEXT 324 1 " " }{TEXT 231 22 "[x1, x2 , . . . , xn
] " }{TEXT 324 6 "where " }{TEXT 232 18 "x1, x2, . . . , xn" }{TEXT 
324 25 " are elements of the set " }{TEXT 233 5 "\{0,1\}" }{TEXT 324 
57 ".  The correspondence is given by associating to the set " }{TEXT 
234 1 "S" }{TEXT 324 10 " the list " }{TEXT 235 1 "L" }{TEXT 324 7 " w
here " }{TEXT 236 34 "xi = 1 if i is in S and 0 if not. " }{TEXT 324 
78 "For example, the set \{1,3,5\} corresponds to the list [1,0,1,0,1,
0,0] if n = 7." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 237 3 "(a)" 
}{TEXT 324 19 " Write a procedure " }{TEXT 238 12 "list_to_set " }
{TEXT 324 79 "whose input is a binary list and whose output is the cor
responding set. E. g., " }{TEXT 239 24 "list_to_set([1,0,1,0,1])" }
{TEXT 324 21 " will return the set " }{TEXT 240 7 "\{1,3,5\}" }{TEXT 
324 44 ". Note that nops(L) is the length of a list." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 241 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 324 19 " Write a procedur
e " }{TEXT 242 11 "set_to_list" }{TEXT 324 22 " whose input is a pair"
 }{TEXT 243 4 " S,n" }{TEXT 324 7 " where " }{TEXT 244 1 "S" }{TEXT 
324 16 " is a subset of " }{TEXT 245 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 
324 5 " and " }{TEXT 246 1 "n" }{TEXT 324 69 " is a positive integer a
nd whose output is the binary list of length " }{TEXT 247 1 "n" }{TEXT
 324 26 " corresponding to the set " }{TEXT 248 1 "S" }{TEXT 324 23 ".
 E. g., if n = 5 then " }{TEXT 249 22 "set_to_list(\{1,3,5\},5)" }
{TEXT 324 13 " will return " }{TEXT 250 11 "[1,0,1,0,1]" }{TEXT 324 2 
".\n" }{TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 251 3 "(c)" }{TEXT 324 59 " Show by a f
ew tests that each procedure works. Then apply " }{TEXT 252 11 "set_to
_list" }{TEXT 324 32 " to each set in the powerset of " }{TEXT 253 12 
"\{1, 2, 3, 4\}" }{TEXT 324 158 " to form all binary lists of length 4
. Make a program to print out a table of the following form. (But the \+
order need not be the same as that started below.)\n" }{TEXT 324 1 "\n
" }{TEXT 324 24 "   [0,0,0,0] <-->  \{  \}\n" }{TEXT 324 24 "   [1,0,0
 0] <--> \{ 1 \}\n" }{TEXT 324 24 "   [0,1,0,0] <--> \{ 2 \} " }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 12 "    ........" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
324 7 "    etc" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 324 108 "Some extra commas in the output is okay. You may obtain
 the power set of the set \{1,2,...,n\} by the command " }{TEXT 254 
12 "powerset(n);" }{TEXT 324 37 " but you must first load the package 
" }{TEXT 255 9 "combinat." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
27 "list_to_set:=proc(n::list)\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "local a, S, i;\n" 
}{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "a:=nops(n);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "S:=\{\};\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "for i to a do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "if n[i]=1 then S:=
 S union \{i\}; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
10 "return S;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "list_to_set([1,0,1,0,1]);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "<%\"\"\"\"\"$\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 9 "restart:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "set_to_list:=proc(s::set,n)\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "local i, L;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "#a:=nops(s);\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "#b:=max(s);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "#L:=[];\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 22 "for i from 1 to n do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "  if i in s then L[
i]:=1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "  else L[i]:= 0; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "return [seq(L[i],i=1..n)];\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0
 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "set_to_list(\{1,5,7\},10);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 4 "[r
];" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "r:=[1,2,3,4,5];\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "has([r],2);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "7,\"\"\"
\"\"!F$F$F#F$F#F$F$F$" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "7#I\"rG6\"" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"rG6\"7'\"\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"%\"\"&" }
}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "I%trueG%*protectedG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 256 11 "Problem  4." }{TEXT 324 1 " " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 13 "Note that if " }{TEXT 
257 1 "i" }{TEXT 324 20 " is an integer then " }{TEXT 258 18 "convert(
i,base, 2)" }{TEXT 324 14 " will convert " }{TEXT 259 2 "i " }{TEXT 
324 47 "to a binary list (the binary representation of " }{TEXT 260 1 
"i" }{TEXT 324 37 " in reverse order). Then you may use " }{TEXT 261 
11 "list_to_set" }{TEXT 324 46 " to convert  this list to a set. Note \+
that if " }{TEXT 262 1 "i" }{TEXT 324 11 " runs from " }{TEXT 263 1 "0
" }{TEXT 324 4 " to " }{XPPEDIT 205 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-
mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"2\", f
ont_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), Typ
esetting:-mi(\"n\", italic = \"true\", font_style_name = \"CharacterSt
yle256\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), superscriptshift = \"0\"), Typese
tting:-mo(\"&minus;\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathva
riant = \"normal\", fence = \"false\", separator = \"false\", stretchy
 = \"false\", symmetric = \"false\", largeop = \"false\", movablelimit
s = \"false\", accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0.2222222em\", rspace = \+
\"0.2222222em\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"1\", font_style_name = \"Character
Style256\", mathvariant = \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%m
rowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6%
-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q\"2F'/%0font_style_nameGQ2CharacterStyle256F'/%
,mathvariantGQ'normalF'-F,6&Q\"nF'/%'italicGQ%trueF'F8/F<Q'italicF'/%1
superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'-I#moGF$6.Q(&minus;F'F8F;/%&fenceGQ&falseF'/%*s
eparatorGFO/%)stretchyGFO/%*symmetricGFO/%(largeopGFO/%.movablelimitsG
FO/%'accentGFO/%'lspaceGQ,0.2222222emF'/%'rspaceGFhn-F56%Q\"1F'F8F;F+"
 }{TEXT 324 6 " then " }{TEXT 264 32 "list_to_set(convert(i,base,2) ) 
" }{TEXT 324 33 " will run through all subsets of " }{TEXT 265 16 "\{1
, 2, . . ., n\}" }{TEXT 324 3 ". \n" }{TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 266 3 "(
a)" }{TEXT 324 42 " Use this idea to make your own procedure " }{TEXT 
267 9 "PowerSet " }{TEXT 324 17 "which will given " }{TEXT 268 1 "n" }
{TEXT 324 27 ",  produce the powerset of " }{TEXT 269 17 "\{1, 2, . . \+
. , n\}" }{TEXT 324 87 ". Show by several examples that your procedure
 works. For your examples you should get " }{TEXT 270 24 "nops(PowerSe
t(n)) = 2^(n" }{TEXT 324 32 "). Check that this is the case.\n" }{TEXT
 324 2 " \n" }{TEXT 271 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 324 35 " Use this idea to make \+
a procedure " }{TEXT 272 7 "RandSet" }{TEXT 324 43 " which given n pro
duces a random subset of " }{TEXT 273 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 
324 7 ". [Use " }{TEXT 274 4 "rand" }{TEXT 324 42 " to produce a rando
m integer in the range " }{TEXT 275 8 "0..2^n-1" }{TEXT 324 36 " and t
hen convert it to a subset of " }{TEXT 276 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }
{TEXT 324 1 "." }{TEXT 277 3 "]  " }{TEXT 278 31 "Do NOT use PowerSet \+
or powerset" }{TEXT 324 240 ". Show by examples that it works for smal
l n such as 5, 10, and 20 as well as for large n such as 100. It will \+
even work for n = 1000, but the output will be rather large. On averag
e a random subset of \{1,2,...,n\} will contain n/2 elements." }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 279 7 "[Remark" }{TEXT 324 62 ": The powerset of
 a set with 1000 elements contains more than " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typeset
ting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(
Typesetting:-mn(\"10\", executable = \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal
\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"300\", executable = \"false\", mathvariant = \"
normal\"), superscriptshift = \"0\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%mr
owG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6#-
I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q#10F'/%+executableGQ&falseF'/%,mathvariantGQ'nor
malF'-F56%Q$300F'F8F;/%1superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'F+" }{TEXT 324 171 " el
ements. This is, according to current estimates, more than the number \+
of atoms in the whole universe. So you cannot expect your computer to \+
be able to store such a set." }{TEXT 280 1 "]" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 10 "Problem
