turns " }{TEXT 317 5 "false" }{TEXT 324 70 ". Find the first 50 such h
appy numbers. Print them out in a sequence. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 318 5 "Hints" }{TEXT 324 1 ":" }{TEXT
 319 1 " " }{TEXT 324 10 " Note that" }{TEXT 320 20 " convert(x,base,1
0) " }{TEXT 324 139 "will give you the decimal digits of x in a list. \+
It should help if you first write a procedure to compute f(x) for any \+
positive integer x. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 205 11 "Problem 5. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 256 "Show that if you incre
ase the upper bound for n in Problem 4  from 20 to a higher value you \+
will not obtain any additional happy numbers less than 320. This also \+
implies that the previously computed 50 happy numbers are the first 50
 happy numbers, indeed. " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "
" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 " " }{TEXT 321 5 "Hints" }{TEXT 324 31 ": Show that
 if in the sequence " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 324 1 "\n" }{TEXT 324 
51 "             n, f(n), (f@@2)(n), . . ., (f@@20)(n)\n" }}{PARA 0 ""
 0 "" {TEXT 205 249 "a 4 is reached before a 1 is reached then a 1 wil
l never be reached, so the number n will not be happy. What happens to
 the sequence once 4 is reached? Look at some examples. Then show that
 in 20 iterations a 1 or a 4 is reached for n less than 320." }{TEXT 
205 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}
